@inject IJSRuntime Js

@*
    One task, as a row: a circle that completes it, the line itself, the two things
    you can do to its text — rename it in place, or copy it — and, on the end, a bin
    that takes the whole row away.

    The circle is a real checkbox to a screen reader and a circle to everyone
    else, because that is what it is — round rather than square is this shape's
    one convention, and it is not worth losing the semantics over. The pencil, the
    copy button and the bin all stop the click before the row sees it: renaming a
    task is not opening it, and neither is copying one or deleting one.

    The bin is last, after even the host's own action slot, because it is the only
    control here whose result is that there is no row. What deleting means is the
    host's — see OnDelete — and a row nobody is listening to has no bin at all,
    which is what keeps it off the lists that have no answer for it.

    There are two handles on the row and they start two different gestures. The
    grip picks the row up to move it; the link handle picks it up to make it wait
    for another row. Order and dependency are independent facts about two rows, so
    the gesture that writes each of them has to be aimable separately — and it is
    aimed by the press, not by where the pointer ends up. Which gesture is in
    flight, and whether this row will take its drop, are the list's answers: see
    LinkHandle and LinkRole. A row cannot see what is being dragged over it.

    Read-only when nothing is listening. A row with no OnToggle renders its state
    as an image rather than a control, the same rule MarkdownView follows for a
    checklist — a checkbox that silently does nothing is worse than no checkbox.
    A blocked row goes the same way even when someone is listening: the circle is
    state, never a button. Offering to complete a task whose predecessor is still
    outstanding would be offering to lie about it.

    A body, when there is one, folds in place under the row rather than opening
    somewhere else. The whole reason to put a prompt on a task is to read it
    where the task is; sending the reader to another surface for it would make
    the list a table of contents for itself.

    Editing that body is a toggle inside the fold, off to begin with, and offered
    only when a host is listening. Reading is the common case, and a list of ten
    prompts that opened ten textareas is not a list anybody skims — so the
    rendered body is what a reader gets until they ask for the other one, exactly
    the way the title stays text until the pencil is pressed. With nobody
    listening there is no toggle and no editor at all, rather than a disabled
    one: this row does not offer controls it cannot honour.

    Every keystroke is reported, and there is no Save button and no Escape. That
    is the opposite of the rename directly above, deliberately, and the whole
    argument is on OnBodyChanged.

    DirectRename turns the title into that same field from the start, for a surface
    whose subject is this one row. It also takes the row's own draggable away, which
    is why the grip is draggable in its own right and Alt+Arrow is heard inside the
    field: a title that is always an input cannot share the pointer with a drag, and
    a row that could only be reordered by pointer would be a row some people cannot
    reorder at all.

    Quick edit is the same rename with Tab wired into it: the key commits this
    row and asks for the next one, so a reader retitling a list types titles
    instead of hunting pencils. Which row that lands on is the list's answer and
    arrives back as Editing — a row cannot see the rows around it, so it cannot
    work out its own successor. Everything the pencil already decided still holds
    inside it: the draft is the row's, Escape abandons it, Enter settles it, and
    an empty or unchanged title raises nothing, so a row tabbed straight through
    is a row nothing happened to. Escape also puts the focus ring back on the
    pencil it came from, which is what a dismissal owes its trigger anywhere —
    and here it is also what stops the end of a chain being a dead end.

    Editing is an invitation and not a state this row is held in. The list asks
    for the editor to open; putting it down is the row's, because only the row
    knows whether there is a draft in it and what its keys promised to do with
    one. That is why the row reports EditingChanged rather than waiting to be
    told: a list left thinking a field is open would refuse to open it again.

    Three ways in, one field, and one predicate deciding whether it is open — see
    RenameOpen. Everything that has to stand out of the field's way asks that one
    question instead of testing the three ways in, which is what keeps the pencil,
    the row's draggable and the line the title would otherwise be from disagreeing
    about whether there is a field there.
*@

@* data-draggable rather than the browser's own draggable, and no drag handlers
   here at all: the whole gesture is pointer events, driven from `taskListDrag` in
   components.js and committed through TaskListView. The argument for leaving
   native drag behind is there rather than repeated here — in short, one of the
   heads this library ships to kills a native drag five milliseconds after it
   starts, so the events a row could bind are never delivered.

   It stays on while a field is open, which the browser's draggable could not: a
   draggable row swallowed the pointer inside its own input, so press-and-move to
   select a word started a drag of the row instead and renaming in place became
   impossible with a mouse. The pointer path has no such conflict — it declines a
   press that lands in the rename field, and the grip goes on working.

   aria-grabbed describes what the row *is* rather than which element the pointer
   has to start on. *@
<li class="@ClassValue"
    data-testid="@TestId"
    data-task-id="@Task.Id"
    data-draggable="@(Draggable ? "true" : null)"
    aria-grabbed="@(Draggable ? (Dragging ? "true" : "false") : null)">

    @if (CanSelect)
    {
        @* Ahead of everything, including the grip: a gutter is a gutter, and a
           column of boxes that started at three different x positions depending
           on whether the row was draggable would not read as one.

           Checkbox, and a square one. The circle beside it is round because
           finishing a task is this product's one shape convention; picking a row
           out to be acted on with others is the plainest checkbox in the product
           and looks it, which is the whole of how a reader tells the two apart.

           Its own event rather than the box's CheckedChanged, because the
           gesture carries a modifier: Shift is what turns one press into a range,
           and a ChangeEventArgs has no keys on it. The click is what the box's
           own change would have followed anyway — a checkbox fires click before
           change — so nothing is lost by hearing the earlier one.

           No keyboard handling of its own, deliberately. The row's Tab
           hand-off is raised from the circle and from the line, and a third
           control claiming Shift+Tab would put the circle behind a key that no
           longer reaches it. Space still ticks the box, because that is what
           Space does to a checkbox. *@
        <span class="task-item__gutter">
            <Checkbox Checked="@SelectionChecked"
                      CssClass="task-item__select"
                      TestId="@Suffix("select")"
                      aria-label="@($"Select {Task.Title}")"
                      @onclick="OnSelectionClickAsync" />
        </span>
    }

    @if (Draggable)
    {
        @* A handle as well as the row: dragging the whole row is what a pointer
           expects, and a handle is what says the row can be dragged at all. It
           is aria-hidden and unfocusable because it is the pointer's affordance
           only — the keyboard reorders with Alt+Arrow on the row itself, so a
           second tab stop here would be a control that duplicates one that is
           already reachable.

           It is also the only place a finger or a pen may start the gesture, and
           the one part of the row a press always claims for the drag: a press
           anywhere else is declined while a field is open, and on a touch screen
           a press anywhere else is how the list is scrolled. No handler of its
           own — the pointer listeners are on the document, and they find the row
           this sits in. *@
        <span class="task-item__grip" aria-hidden="true">⠿</span>
    }

    @if (CanToggle)
    {
        @* The circle is the row's first tab stop, so it is where a reader tabbing
           backwards leaves the row — and it takes an id of its own for the same
           reason the body does: a host that has to put the focus on the start of
           this row has to be able to name it.

           "Complete Wire up the store" rather than "Wire up the store", the way
           the pencil and the bin name themselves: the line beside this circle is
           already called after the task, so a circle called the same thing gave
           one row two controls answering to one name — and left a reader with no
           way to tell the one that finishes the task from the one that opens it.
           A selector reaching for the line by that name found this button
           instead, which is how a pass that meant to open a task completed it.

           The act, not the state: aria-checked is what says whether it is done,
           and a name that changed with it would be a control that renamed itself
           under the reader. *@
        <button type="button"
                class="task-item__check"
                role="checkbox"
                id="@CheckElementId"
                aria-checked="@(Task.Done ? "true" : "false")"
                aria-label="@($"Complete {Task.Title}")"
                data-testid="@Suffix("check")"
                @onkeydown="OnCheckKeyDownAsync"
                @onclick="() => OnToggle.InvokeAsync(Task.Id)"
                @onclick:stopPropagation="true">
            @if (Task.Done)
            {
                <span class="task-item__tick" aria-hidden="true">✓</span>
            }
        </button>
    }
    else
    {
        <span class="task-item__check"
              role="img"
              aria-label="@StateLabel"
              data-testid="@Suffix("check")">
            @if (Task.Done)
            {
                <span class="task-item__tick" aria-hidden="true">✓</span>
            }
        </span>
    }

    @if (RenameOpen)
    {
        @* Committed on Enter or on leaving, abandoned on Escape. A rename is one
           short string, so a Save button beside it would be a second thing to
           reach for after the one that already finished the job.

           The same field serves the pencil, DirectRename and the list's own
           request. Three implementations of one control diverge, and the first
           thing to diverge would have been which keys commit.

           Under quick edit, Tab settles it too — and there is deliberately no
           @onkeydown:preventDefault here: it is read now, before anybody has
           pressed anything, so the only constant that could catch a Tab would
           also swallow every letter typed into the field. The browser is stopped
           from moving the focus on by backlogGuardTab instead. *@
        <div class="task-item__rename @(DirectRename ? "task-item__rename--direct" : null)">
            @* data-testid goes on the input rather than through TestId, which
               lives on a wrapper Bare does not render — so the name went nowhere
               and the field could only be found by its class. *@
            <TextField Value="@_draft"
                       ValueChanged="v => _draft = v"
                       AriaLabel="@($"Rename {Task.Title}")"
                       Bare="true"
                       InputCssClass="task-item__rename-input"
                       data-testid="@Suffix("rename")"
                       Id="@RenameFieldId"
                       @onkeydown="OnRenameKeyAsync"
                       @onblur="CommitRenameAsync" />

            @RenderStatus()
        </div>

        @* Beside the field rather than gone. The metadata line lives inside the
           line the field replaces, so a row whose title is always a field would
           otherwise have quietly stopped saying when it is due or what it waits
           on — the same facts, dropped by the choice of how to edit the title. *@
        @RenderMeta()
    }
    else if (MetaOutside)
    {
        @* The metadata line leaves the button, and takes a box with it.

           It has to leave: its tags are controls now, and a button inside a button
           is not a shape the DOM has — bUnit's parser hoists the inner one, so the
           test would stop describing the browser. Leaving the tags inert instead
           would put the only way back to "All" behind a chip the narrow bar hides,
           and hoisting the tags alone would split one line of metadata in two.

           What it must not do is change the row. The button was a two-row grid —
           title and status on the first, this line spanning both columns on the
           second — and a line promoted to a sibling of it is a second item on the
           row's own flex line, which pushed the pickers off the end and onto a
           third line of their own. The wrapper is that grid's second row given
           back: one flex item where the button used to be, holding the button and
           the line under it, so the row is the same height, the line starts at the
           same left edge, and the pickers stay where they were. *@
        <div class="task-item__line">
            @RenderOpen()
            @RenderMeta()
        </div>
    }
    else
    {
        @RenderOpen()
    }

    @if (LinkHandle)
    {
        @* The second handle: press this and move, and the row is being made to
           wait for whichever row it is dropped on rather than moved.

           It is a handle and not a drop zone, and that distinction is the whole
           design. A drop zone inside the row meant the mode of the gesture was
           whatever the pointer happened to be over at the release, which turned
           out to be bistable — the retracting reorder preview reflowed the list
           out from under the aimed-at row, and the two modes traded places for as
           long as the reader held still. A handle settles the question before the
           gesture starts: see TaskListView's _linking, and taskRowFromPoint in
           components.js. The drop target is the whole of the other row.

           Visible at rest rather than on hover, for the reason the copy button is:
           an affordance that only appears under the pointer cannot be found by
           anyone looking for it. Quiet until the row is reached, so a column of
           these does not read as the point of the list.

           First of the row's controls, ahead of the pencil. It is the other half
           of the grip at the row's other end — the two things you can pick a row
           up by — and a handle you drag belongs beside what you are dragging
           rather than filed among the buttons that act on it. Everything after it
           is a click; this one is not.

           aria-hidden and no tab stop, exactly as the grip is and for the same
           reason: it is the pointer's affordance, and the keyboard has a route of
           its own in Alt+Shift+Arrow. No handler either — the pointer listeners
           are on the document and they find the row this sits in.

           The glyph is a link and not the hourglass the metadata line wears: the
           line says a row *is* waiting, which is a state, and this says a chain
           can be drawn from here, which is a gesture. Wearing the state's mark on
           a control would promise that pressing it is what makes a row wait. *@
        <span class="task-item__link"
              aria-hidden="true"
              data-testid="@Suffix("link")">🔗</span>
    }

    @if (CanRename && !RenameOpen)
    {
        <button type="button"
                class="task-item__edit"
                id="@_editId"
                aria-label="@($"Rename {Task.Title}")"
                data-testid="@Suffix("edit")"
                @onclick="StartRenameAsync"
                @onclick:stopPropagation="true">
            <span aria-hidden="true">✎</span>
        </button>
    }

    @if (Actions is not null)
    {
        @* What the host can do to this task that this library has no opinion
           about: push it somewhere, hand it to a tool, open it in an editor. The
           row cannot know what those are, and a host that had nowhere to put them
           would build a second row beside this one to hold them.

           Before the copy button rather than after it. Everything from the circle
           to here says what the task is and where it has got to — including the
           state a host puts in this slot — and copying is the one act on the row
           that takes that away with you. It reads last of the things that leave
           the row standing, so the reader settles the task and then lifts it.

           Clicks stop here for the same reason the pencil stops them: pushing a
           task is not opening it. *@
        <span class="task-item__actions"
              role="group"
              aria-label="@($"Actions for {Task.Title}")"
              @onmousedown:stopPropagation="true"
              @onclick:stopPropagation="true">
            @Actions(Task)
        </span>
    }

    @if (AllowCopy)
    {
        @* Behind the state, and behind the host's own slot with it. This is the
           one control on the row that acts on the task's text rather than on what
           the task is, and a reader reaches for it once the rest of the row has
           told them this is the task they wanted — so it sits after the answer
           rather than in front of the question.

           Still ahead of the bin. Everything to the left of the bin leaves the row
           where it is. *@
        <CopyButton Text="@CopyText"
                    Label="@($"Copy {Task.Title}")"
                    CssClass="task-item__copy"
                    ButtonCssClass="task-item__copy-button"
                    StatusCssClass="task-item__copy-status"
                    ButtonTestId="@Suffix("copy")"
                    StatusTestId="@Suffix("copy-status")" />
    }

    @if (CanDelete)
    {
        @* Last on the row, after everything that acts on the task and after the
           host's own slot. Everything to the left of here does something to the row;
           this one ends it, and a destructive control sitting between two ordinary
           ones is a control the pointer finds by accident.

           Offered only when a host is listening, exactly like the pencil — which is
           what keeps the bin off the lists that do not want one, since a list that
           passes no OnDelete passes none of its rows one either.

           The click stops here. Deleting a task is not opening one, which is the
           same reason the pencil and the copy button stop theirs.

           DeleteButton, bare, rather than a button of this row's own. It was a bare
           emoji here, which is the thing that component was added to retire: a 🗑
           renders differently on every platform and carries no colour of its own,
           so the one irreversible act on the row was drawn by whatever font the
           reader happened to have. The glyph is drawn now, and the row goes on
           dressing the square by name — `.task-item__delete` is what the stylesheet
           quietens until the row is reached and what components.js keeps out of the
           row-drag gesture.

           The glyph is aria-hidden and the label carries the meaning, naming the row
           the way the pencil names itself: "Delete Wire up the store" rather than
           "Delete", so a screen reader reading down a column of these says which
           one each of them is. *@
        <DeleteButton Bare="true"
                      BaseClass="task-item__delete"
                      AriaLabel="@($"Delete {Task.Title}")"
                      TestId="@Suffix("delete")"
                      StopPropagation="true"
                      OnClick="() => OnDelete.InvokeAsync(Task.Id)" />
    }

    @if (HasFold)
    {
        @* FoldControl rather than a disclosure of this row's own: it already
           owns the trigger's aria-expanded, the id the trigger points at, and
           keeping the region in the DOM while it is hidden. A second
           implementation of that would be a second one to get wrong.

           It takes a line of its own under the row. The title line keeps the
           shape every other row in the list has, and the body arrives below it
           rather than pushing the copy button off the end. *@
        <FoldControl @bind-Expanded="_expanded"
                     Label="@BodyLabel"
                     AriaLabel="@($"{BodyLabel} for {Task.Title}")"
                     BaseClass="task-item__fold"
                     TestId="@Suffix("body-toggle")">
            @if (CanEditBody)
            {
                @* Inside the region rather than beside the title, so it is
                   reachable exactly when the body it edits is on screen — and so
                   the title line keeps the shape every other row has.

                   aria-pressed rather than two labels: the button does the same
                   thing to the same body either way, and "Edit Prompt" that
                   became "Stop editing Prompt" would be a control whose name
                   changes under the reader between one press and the next. *@
                <div class="task-item__body-tools">
                    <button type="button"
                            class="task-item__body-edit"
                            aria-pressed="@(_editingBody ? "true" : "false")"
                            data-testid="@Suffix("body-edit")"
                            @onclick="() => _editingBody = !_editingBody"
                            @onclick:stopPropagation="true">
                        <span aria-hidden="true">✎</span>
                        <span>Edit @BodyLabel</span>
                    </button>
                </div>
            }

            @if (_editingBody)
            {
                @* MarkdownEditor, not a textarea of this row's own. A body here is
                   prose written by the same person in the same product as every
                   other body, and a second editor would be a second set of
                   keystrokes to get right.

                   Value comes straight off the Task the host handed over. There
                   is no draft: what the reader sees typed is what the host has
                   already been told. *@
                <MarkdownEditor Value="@Task.Body"
                                ValueChanged="OnBodyTypedAsync"
                                AriaLabel="@($"{BodyLabel} for {Task.Title}")"
                                ToolbarLabel="@($"Formatting for {Task.Title}")"
                                Rows="4"
                                AutoGrow="true"
                                CssClass="task-item__body-editor"
                                TestId="@Suffix("body-editor")" />
            }
            else
            {
                <div class="task-item__body-text" data-testid="@Suffix("body")">
                    <MarkdownView Blocks="@BodyBlocks" />
                </div>
            }
        </FoldControl>
    }
</li>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// Where the row has got to, beside its title.
    /// <para>
    /// On the title's line rather than in the metadata line below it, because a
    /// status is what the title is currently doing — the same reason
    /// <see cref="TaskPanel"/> puts it beside the heading, drawn by the same
    /// <see cref="StatusBadge"/>. A reader who opens a row from a list should find
    /// the fact in the same shape and the same place they just left it in.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// A fragment because it is drawn twice, for the reason
    /// <see cref="RenderMeta"/> is: a row whose title has become a field must not
    /// stop saying what state it is in.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private RenderFragment RenderStatus() =>
        @<text>
        @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Task.Status))
        {
            <StatusBadge Status="@Task.Status" CssClass="task-item__status" TestId="@Suffix("status")" />
        }
        </text>;

    /// <summary>The row's own focusable element, and so where Alt+Arrow is heard.
    /// It carries an id because after a move this is a different element and the
    /// focus has to be put back on the row that moved rather than on whatever slid
    /// into its old place.
    /// <para>
    /// A fragment because there are two places it is drawn from — bare, and inside
    /// the wrapper that carries a displaced metadata line — and a button written
    /// twice is a keyboard contract written twice.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// It says what it does in a word only a screen reader reads, and not in an
    /// <c>aria-label</c>. A label on this button would <em>be</em> its whole name,
    /// and the name is currently everything inside it — the title, the status
    /// badge, and the metadata line that goes to the trouble of writing out what
    /// its glyphs mean so a reader hears "Reminder 09:00" rather than a bell. All
    /// of that would have gone out of the accessibility tree to buy one word.
    /// Putting the word in the content instead keeps the row's facts in its name
    /// and still tells a reader which of the row's two constant controls this is:
    /// the circle completes the task, this opens it.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// So the name is not a fixed string, and nothing should reach for this button
    /// by one. <c>data-testid</c> is the handle — <c>&lt;test-id&gt;-open</c>,
    /// which is what every test and every host already uses.
    /// </para></summary>
    private RenderFragment RenderOpen() =>
        @<button type="button"
                 class="task-item__body"
                 id="@RowElementId"
                 data-testid="@Suffix("open")"
                 @onkeydown="OnRowKeyDownAsync"
                 @onclick="() => OnSelected.InvokeAsync(Task.Id)">
            @* First, so the name opens with the act rather than ending on it:
               "Open Wire up the store, due today" is a reader's sentence, and
               a reader skimming a column of rows hears which control this is
               before the title they are skimming for. *@
            <span class="sr-only">Open</span>

            <span class="task-item__title" data-testid="@Suffix("title")">@RenderTitle()</span>

            @RenderStatus()

            @* Inside unless it has been moved out; see the branch that moves it. *@
            @if (!MetaOutside)
            {
                @RenderMeta()
            }
        </button>;

    /// <summary>
    /// The title, with any tags typed into it drawn as chips where they stand.
    ///
    /// <para>
    /// Nothing is stripped and nothing is reordered: the segments concatenate back
    /// to the title character for character, so the accessible name of the button
    /// still carries the whole title a person typed — <c>Ship the installer @bob
    /// #deploy</c>, sigils and all, after the word that says the button opens it.
    /// The chips are a reading of the text, not an edit of it.
    /// </para>
    ///
    /// <para>
    /// The chips here are always inert, even when a host is listening for tags,
    /// and that is not a decision so much as an arithmetic: the title <em>is</em>
    /// the button that opens the row, and a button cannot hold a button. The
    /// pressable copy of every tag is already on the metadata line below.
    /// </para>
    ///
    /// <para>
    /// Display only. The title also feeds the rename draft, the row's aria-labels
    /// and the copy payload, and every one of those keeps the raw string: a chip
    /// is something a reader sees, never something an editor or the clipboard
    /// inherits.
    /// </para></summary>
    private RenderFragment RenderTitle() =>
        @<text>
        @foreach (var segment in TagText.Segments(Task.Title))
        {
            if (segment.IsTag)
            {
                <TagChip Text="@segment.Text" CssClass="task-item__title-tag" />
            }
            else
            {
                @segment.Text
            }
        }
        </text>;

    /// <summary>
    /// Everything the row knows about itself, in the order a reader asks for it:
    /// its place in the chain, then its own dates and tags.
    /// <para>
    /// A fragment rather than markup written twice, because the line belongs to the
    /// row and not to whichever element happens to be holding the title. It used to
    /// live inside the title button, which meant a row whose title became a field
    /// stopped saying when it was due — the same facts lost to a decision about how
    /// the title is edited.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// Nothing at all when there is nothing to say. An empty span here would be a
    /// metadata line about no metadata, and CSS cannot stand in for the test: a
    /// fragment whose branches are all false still leaves whitespace.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private RenderFragment RenderMeta() =>
        @<text>
        @if (Task.TagList.Count > 0 || Details.Count > 0)
        {
            <span class="task-item__meta">
                @foreach (var detail in Details)
                {
                    <span class="@detail.CssClass" title="@detail.Name">
                        @if (detail.Glyph.Length > 0)
                        {
                            @* The glyph is decoration on top of the words:
                               a screen reader hears "Reminder 09:00", not
                               "alarm clock emoji". *@
                            <span class="task-item__glyph" aria-hidden="true">@detail.Glyph</span>
                            <span class="sr-only">@detail.Name</span>
                        }
                        <span>@detail.Text</span>
                    </span>
                }

                @foreach (var tag in Task.TagList)
                {
                    @* Inert unless a host is listening, which is the same rule the
                       circle and the pencil follow: a tag that looked pressable and
                       did nothing would be worse than one that never offered. Both
                       forms are the shared chip: the inert one renders a span
                       rather than a disabled control, so a list that passes no
                       callback still gets no control — but it gets it from the
                       component rather than from a hand-rolled span wearing the
                       component's own class. *@
                    @* The hash is drawn, never stored — but a person tag stores its
                       `@` and must not be given a second sigil on top of it. One
                       helper decides, so the pressable chip, its accessible name
                       and the inert span cannot drift into disagreeing about how a
                       tag is spelled. *@
                    @if (OnTagSelected.HasDelegate)
                    {
                        <TagChip Text="@TagText.Display(tag)"
                                 CssClass="task-item__tag"
                                 Label="@TagText.FilterLabel(tag)"
                                 OnClick="() => OnTagSelected.InvokeAsync(tag)" />
                    }
                    else
                    {
                        <TagChip Text="@TagText.Display(tag)" CssClass="task-item__tag" />
                    }
                }
            </span>
        }
        </text>;

    private readonly string _renameId = "task-rename-" + Guid.NewGuid().ToString("N");

    /// <summary>The pencil, by id, so a dismissed rename can put the focus ring
    /// back where it came from. Named even when nothing is going to ask for it: the
    /// button is one element for the life of the row, so the id costs an attribute
    /// and saves a conditional.</summary>
    private readonly string _editId = "task-edit-" + Guid.NewGuid().ToString("N");
    private bool _editing;
    private string _draft = string.Empty;

    /// <summary>The title the always-open field's draft was seeded from, so a
    /// render caused by something else in the list cannot overwrite what the reader
    /// is typing. Null when the row has no such field.</summary>
    private string? _seededFrom;

    /// <summary>What the list last asked for, so that only a change of mind acts.
    /// Comparing against <c>_editing</c> instead would re-open the field the
    /// moment a reader pressed Escape in it, because the list's request is still
    /// standing at that point — it has not been told yet.</summary>
    private bool _asked;

    /// <summary>Whether the body is open. Held here rather than raised, because
    /// which rows a reader has unfolded is not a fact about the tasks — it dies
    /// with the view, and a host asked to store it would be storing scroll
    /// position by another name.</summary>
    private bool _expanded;

    /// <summary>Whether the open body is being edited rather than read. Held here
    /// for the same reason the fold is: which row a reader happens to be typing
    /// in is not a fact about the tasks. Off to start with, always — the common
    /// case is reading, and a list that opened every editor at once would be a
    /// list of textareas.</summary>
    private bool _editingBody;

    /// <summary>The field has just been opened and has not been focused yet.
    /// A request rather than a state, and cleared the first time it is honoured:
    /// the caret belongs in the field once, when it appears, and a row that put it
    /// back on every render would fight whoever is typing in it.</summary>
    private bool _focusRename;

    /// <summary>The rename has just been dismissed and the focus ring has not been
    /// put back yet. The same shape as <see cref="_focusRename"/> deliberately —
    /// one mechanism for "after the next render, touch this element", because the
    /// element in both cases is one a render has to create first.</summary>
    private bool _focusEdit;

    private IReadOnlyList<MdBlock>? _blocks;
    private string? _parsed;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public TaskRow Task { get; set; } = new(string.Empty, string.Empty);

    /// <summary>Raised by the circle. Without it the circle is state, not a
    /// control.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<string> OnToggle { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Whether the row offers to copy itself. On by default: a task's
    /// text is the thing people paste into a message, a commit or another
    /// list, and reaching for it otherwise means selecting a line that is also a
    /// click target.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool AllowCopy { get; set; } = true;

    /// <summary>
    /// What lands on the clipboard. Set it and it wins outright: a host that has
    /// said what this row pastes as knows something this component does not.
    /// <para>
    /// Unset, it is the title — the row's metadata is this list's furniture
    /// rather than the task, and pasting "Tasks · Today" into a message says
    /// nothing.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// Unset on a row that has a body, it is the title, a blank line, and the
    /// body. The body is the thing people came to paste, and pasting it alone
    /// hands over a headless paragraph that no longer says which step it was —
    /// the title is what makes it findable again afterwards.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? CopyValue { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The host's own controls for this row, handed the task they are about.
    /// <para>
    /// A slot rather than a set of parameters, because what a host wants to do to
    /// a task is not a list this library can finish. Unset, the row renders exactly
    /// the markup it rendered before the slot existed — an empty group would be an
    /// element with an aria-label about nothing.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public RenderFragment<TaskRow>? Actions { get; set; }

    /// <summary>What the disclosure calls the body. "Body" by default, because
    /// this library does not know what a host writes there — a prompt, a brief,
    /// a note — and naming it after one of those would put the product's
    /// vocabulary inside a component that has none of its own.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string BodyLabel { get; set; } = "Body";

    /// <summary>Whether the body starts open. Read once, as the row first
    /// renders: after that the fold belongs to the reader, and a parameter that
    /// kept re-asserting itself would snap the body shut under them every time
    /// anything else in the list moved.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool BodyExpanded { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// This row's place in the chain, as the list worked it out.
    /// <para>
    /// Passed in rather than derived here, because deriving it needs the other
    /// rows and a row cannot see them. Null when nothing in the list declared a
    /// dependency, and then this row renders exactly what it rendered before
    /// chains existed.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public TaskChainStatus? Status { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Whether this is the row to pick up next. A fact about the list
    /// rather than about the task, which is why the list sets it and the row
    /// only wears it.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool Next { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Raised by the row itself — "show me this one".</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<string> OnSelected { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Raised when one of the row's tags is pressed, carrying the bare tag — no
    /// leading <c>#</c>, which is drawn rather than stored.
    /// <para>
    /// Unset, the tags stay the plain text they have always been. A host that wants
    /// them to lead somewhere — to a filter, usually — says so, and only then does
    /// the row spend a tab stop per tag and move its metadata line out of the button
    /// that opens it. What pressing one <em>means</em> is the host's: this row knows
    /// which tag was pressed and nothing about what is on the other side of it.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<string> OnTagSelected { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Whether the metadata line is drawn beside the row's button rather
    /// than inside it. True exactly when there are tags and they are controls —
    /// nothing else needs the line moved, and a row with no tags keeps the layout
    /// every other list has.</summary>
    private bool MetaOutside => OnTagSelected.HasDelegate && Task.TagList.Count > 0;

    /// <summary>Raised when a rename settles. Without it the row offers no way
    /// in — a pencil that opened a field nobody would save is worse than none.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<TaskRename> OnRename { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Raised by the bin at the end of the row: this task, by id.
    /// <para>
    /// The id and nothing else, on the terms <see cref="OnToggle"/> and
    /// <see cref="OnSelected"/> already set. What deleting a task means — whether
    /// it is a row removed from a list, a chapter cut out of a document, or a
    /// soft delete with a way back — is the host's, exactly as saving a rename and
    /// applying a reorder are.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// It is also what puts the bin on the row at all. Without it there is no
    /// control, rather than a disabled one: a row does not offer to delete itself
    /// where nobody would hear, and the lists in this product that have no answer
    /// to "delete this" simply do not pass it.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<string> OnDelete { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether the title is a field from the start, with no pencil to press first.
    /// <para>
    /// Off by default, because a list of ten rows that opened ten inputs is not a
    /// list anybody skims — the same argument the body editor makes one parameter
    /// further down. On for a row that is the subject of the surface it is on: a
    /// detail pane showing one entry has nothing to scan, so a pencil there is a
    /// press between the reader and a title they came to change.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// It settles on exactly the same terms as the pencil's field — Enter and blur
    /// commit, Escape puts it back, an empty or unchanged title raises nothing —
    /// because a rename that meant two different things depending on how the field
    /// opened would be two controls wearing one name.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// Nothing without <see cref="OnRename"/>. A field nobody is listening to is
    /// this component's definition of a control that lies, and here it would be one
    /// the reader cannot even avoid opening.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool DirectRename { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether Tab settles the rename and asks for the next row instead of
    /// leaving the field. Off by default, and that is not caution: Tab means "the
    /// next control" in every other field in the product, and a list that quietly
    /// took it over would be a list some people cannot tab out of.
    /// <para>
    /// The list turns it on for every row at once. Whether Tab chains is a fact
    /// about the list being retitled rather than about any one task in it, and a
    /// row that decided for itself would make the key mean two different things
    /// on two rows of the same list.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool QuickEdit { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The list asking this row to be the one with the editor open — how a Tab
    /// pressed in the row above arrives here, since that row could not know which
    /// row it was handing on to.
    /// <para>
    /// An invitation rather than a state. Turning it on opens the field and puts
    /// the caret in it; turning it off does nothing, because by then the row has
    /// already closed the field itself. A list that could shut the editor from
    /// outside would be a list that can throw away a draft the row promised
    /// Escape was the only way to lose.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// Ignored when nobody is listening to <see cref="OnRename"/>, on exactly the
    /// terms the pencil is: a field whose rename would go nowhere is worse than
    /// no field, whoever asked for it.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool Editing { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Raised when the row opens or closes its editor, so the list's idea
    /// of which row is being edited is the row's. Without it a list would go on
    /// believing a field it asked for is still open after Escape closed it, and
    /// asking for the same row again — the same request twice — would change
    /// nothing at all.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<bool> EditingChanged { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Raised after a Tab has settled the rename: this row, and which way
    /// the reader was going. It is a question rather than an instruction — the
    /// list owns the order, so only the list can turn "onwards" into a row.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<TaskEditMove> OnEditMove { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Raised on every keystroke in the body, with the whole of it. Without it
    /// the body stays a rendered <c>MarkdownView</c> and no editor is offered at
    /// all — not a disabled one, because a control nobody is listening to is a
    /// control that lies.
    /// <para>
    /// Per keystroke, and that is the opposite of <see cref="OnRename"/> two
    /// parameters up. A title is one line, so Enter can mean "done" and the row
    /// can hold a draft until it does. A body is multi-line markdown where Enter
    /// is a newline, so there is no keystroke left over to mean "done" — and the
    /// product has no save button anywhere, so a body that needed one would be
    /// the single place in it that did.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// Which is also why no Escape is offered here. The rename holds a draft, so
    /// it has something of its own to throw away; this row holds nothing, and the
    /// host already has every character. An Escape that "put it back" would have
    /// to be an undo the host implements, so offering one on the row would be
    /// offering a control that cannot do what its name says.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// Committing on blur was the alternative and it is worse twice over: a
    /// reader who folds the row away or moves on loses the text, and the copy
    /// button beside it would copy a stale body while the editor held newer.
    /// Reporting each keystroke makes <see cref="CopyText"/> correct by
    /// construction instead — the body it formats is the body the reader can see,
    /// because the round trip through the host has already happened.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<TaskBodyChange> OnBodyChanged { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public bool Selected { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether the row carries a selection box in front of it.
    /// <para>
    /// The list's answer and not the row's, exactly like <see cref="Draggable"/>:
    /// picking rows out to act on together is a fact about the list they are in,
    /// and a row that decided for itself would put a gutter on some rows of one
    /// list and not others.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// Nothing without <see cref="SelectionChanged"/>, the rule the circle, the
    /// pencil and the bin all follow — a box whose press would go nowhere is
    /// worse than no box.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool Selectable { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Whether this row is one of the picked ones. Deliberately not
    /// <see cref="Selected"/>: that one means "this is the row whose detail is
    /// open", and one class carrying both meanings would make the row a reader is
    /// reading and the rows they are about to retag look identical.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool SelectionChecked { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Raised when the box is pressed: this row, what it now reads as,
    /// and whether Shift was down. What a range means is the list's, because only
    /// the list can see the rows in between.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<TaskSelectionToggle> SelectionChanged { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Whether this row can be picked up. The list turns it on; a row on
    /// its own has nothing to be reordered within.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool Draggable { get; set; }

    /// <summary>This row is the one being dragged.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool Dragging { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether this row can be picked up to be made to wait for another one, and
    /// so whether it draws the handle that does it.
    /// <para>
    /// The list's answer, on the same terms as <see cref="Draggable"/> and for the
    /// same two reasons: whether anybody is listening for a link is the list's
    /// parameter, and a handle that started a gesture nothing would come of is
    /// worse than no handle. Off, this row's markup is exactly what it was before
    /// links existed.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// Not folded into <see cref="Draggable"/>, which also carries whether the
    /// list reorders at all. A list whose order is not the reader's to choose can
    /// still be a list whose chain is.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool LinkHandle { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// What part this row is playing in the link drag in flight — including
    /// whether releasing on it will do anything.
    /// <para>
    /// The whole row is the drop target, so the row is what has to say yes or no.
    /// There is no sub-region to aim at: the mode of the gesture was settled by
    /// the press that started it, which is what keeps the aiming and the meaning
    /// from arguing with each other mid-drag.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// The list's answer, because every input to it — is a link drag in flight,
    /// is this the row that was picked up, does that row already wait for this
    /// one — is a fact about the rows around this one.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public TaskLinkRole LinkRole { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether the pointer is on this row right now, so it can say it is the one
    /// about to take the drop.
    /// <para>
    /// A parameter rather than <c>:hover</c>. Where the pointer is has already
    /// been round-tripped to decide where the drop lands — see
    /// <c>TaskListView.PointerDragOver</c> — and a second source of truth for it
    /// would be one to get wrong on the frame they disagree. It is also the only
    /// form of "armed" a test can see.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// The class it produces is read back out of the DOM by the script that draws
    /// the link line, which snaps to the row wearing it. That makes this the
    /// published answer to "what will this drop do" rather than one row's
    /// decoration, so a row that wore it without being the drop's target — or a
    /// target that did not wear it — would put the line somewhere the drop is not
    /// going. The list is the only thing that may set it.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// A second axis rather than another <see cref="TaskLinkRole"/>, because it
    /// crosses all of them: the row under the pointer may equally be the one
    /// about to take the drop or the one about to refuse it.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool LinkArmed { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The reader asked for this row to wait for a neighbour: -1 for the one
    /// above, 1 for the one below.
    /// <para>
    /// A direction rather than a row, on the same terms as
    /// <see cref="OnKeyboardMove"/> — this row cannot see its neighbours, and
    /// which of them is a legal thing to wait for is the list's answer.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// Deliberately not gated on <see cref="Draggable"/> the way the move is: a
    /// list whose order is not the reader's to choose can still be a list whose
    /// chain is, and there would be nothing to reorder either way. Nor on whether
    /// this row is finished, even though a finished row has no handle: the chord
    /// has no affordance to withhold, so the only way to tell the reader their
    /// press was heard and refused is to report it and let the list say so.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<int> OnKeyboardLink { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The id to put on the row's focusable element, so a caller that moved this
    /// row can give it the focus back.
    /// <para>
    /// Supplied by the list rather than minted here. After a move the row is a
    /// different element, and only the list knows which row it just moved — a
    /// per-instance id generated in here would be unknowable to the one thing
    /// that needs to name it.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? RowElementId { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The reader asked to move this row: -1 for up, 1 for down.
    /// <para>
    /// A direction rather than a destination, because a row cannot see its
    /// neighbours. The list turns it into a <see cref="TaskMove"/>, which is also
    /// why nothing happens when no list is listening.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<int> OnKeyboardMove { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The reader tabbed forward off this row and a host wants the focus
    /// somewhere other than the next thing in the document.
    /// <para>
    /// A row sits in a list, and what comes after a list in reading order is the
    /// rest of the list. A host showing one row's detail beside the list cannot
    /// be reached by tabbing without walking every remaining row first, and only
    /// the row knows that the reader has just tried — which is why this is the
    /// row's event rather than something a host could listen for itself.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// Nothing is prevented. Blazor resolves <c>preventDefault</c> when the row
    /// renders rather than when the key arrives (see
    /// <see cref="OnRowKeyDownAsync"/>), so suppressing Tab here would suppress
    /// Shift+Tab and Enter with it — a keyboard trap in exchange for a tidier
    /// hand-off. The browser's own move therefore still happens, and the focus a
    /// host sets afterwards is the one that lasts.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback OnTabForward { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The reader tabbed backward off the front of this row: the way back out of
    /// a row a host tabbed them into.
    /// <para>
    /// Raised from the row's <em>first</em> control only — the circle when there
    /// is one, the line itself when there is not. Raising it from the line as
    /// well would put the circle behind a Shift+Tab that no longer goes there and
    /// so out of reach of the keyboard altogether, which is a worse bargain than
    /// one extra stop on the way out.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback OnTabBackward { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? CssClass { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? TestId { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The circle is a control only when there is something for it to
    /// do. Nobody listening, or a row that cannot honestly be completed yet,
    /// and it is state instead.</summary>
    private bool CanToggle => OnToggle.HasDelegate && !Blocked;

    /// <summary>Whether there is a gutter. Both halves matter: the list has to
    /// have asked for one, and something has to be listening to the press —
    /// a box that quietly did nothing is the one shape this component refuses
    /// everywhere else.
    /// <para>
    /// Unlike the circle, a done row is still selectable. Retagging a finished
    /// task or moving a batch of them to another repository is ordinary work, and
    /// a gutter that skipped the Completed section would make it impossible.
    /// </para></summary>
    private bool CanSelect => Selectable && SelectionChanged.HasDelegate;

    /// <summary>The circle's element id, derived from the row's rather than named
    /// separately: a host that supplied one handle for this row should not have to
    /// supply a second one for the control at the start of it.</summary>
    private string? CheckElementId => RowElementId is { Length: > 0 } row ? $"{row}-check" : null;

    /// <summary>Where <see cref="FocusFirstAsync"/> aims: the circle when the row
    /// has one, otherwise the line. Null when the host never named the row, in
    /// which case there is nothing here to name either.</summary>
    private string? FirstControlId =>
        RowElementId is { Length: > 0 } row ? (CanToggle ? CheckElementId : row) : null;

    /// <summary>
    /// Puts the focus on the first control in this row.
    /// <para>
    /// The counterpart to <see cref="OnTabForward"/>: a host that moves the focus
    /// into a region because of one row wants a way to move it onto a row, and the
    /// row's internals — whether the circle is a control today — are exactly what
    /// it should not have to know. Needs <see cref="RowElementId"/>, because the
    /// element to focus is named rather than held: it is a different node after
    /// every render.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    public async Task FocusFirstAsync()
    {
        if (FirstControlId is not { } id) return;

        try
        {
            await Js.InvokeVoidAsync("backlogFocus", id);
        }
        catch (Exception)
        {
            // Losing the focus ring is not worth tearing down the page, and the
            // same call is guarded the same way where a rename opens its field.
        }
    }

    private bool CanRename => OnRename.HasDelegate;

    /// <summary>Whether the row ends in a bin. One term, and it is the delegate:
    /// unlike the rename there is no second way in and no mode to be in, so
    /// "somebody is listening" is the whole question.</summary>
    private bool CanDelete => OnDelete.HasDelegate;

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether the title is a field right now. One predicate, because everything
    /// that has to stand out of the field's way — the pencil, the row's own
    /// draggable, the line the title would otherwise be — asks the same question,
    /// and three separate tests would be three chances to disagree about whether
    /// there is a field there.
    /// <para>
    /// Two terms for three ways in. The pencil sets <c>_editing</c> and so does the
    /// list's <see cref="Editing"/> request, because that request is honoured by
    /// opening the field rather than by standing over it — reading
    /// <see cref="Editing"/> here as well would re-open a field the reader had just
    /// pressed Escape in, since the request outlives the dismissal by exactly one
    /// round trip. <see cref="DirectRename"/> is a term of its own because in that
    /// mode nothing was ever opened.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private bool RenameOpen => _editing || (DirectRename && CanRename);

    /// <summary>
    /// The field's element id.
    /// <para>
    /// The row's own id in direct mode, because in that mode the field <em>is</em>
    /// the row's focusable element: it is what a list has to aim at to give a moved
    /// row its focus back, and a privately minted id would be unknowable to the one
    /// thing that needs to name it. The private one otherwise, where the field is a
    /// temporary thing the pencil opened.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private string RenameFieldId =>
        DirectRename && RowElementId is { Length: > 0 } row ? row : _renameId;

    /// <summary>Whether the body is the reader's to change, on the same terms as
    /// the pencil: somebody is listening, or there is nothing to offer.</summary>
    private bool CanEditBody => OnBodyChanged.HasDelegate;

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether there is a region under the row at all.
    /// <para>
    /// The body, or an edit in progress. Not just the body: a reader who selects
    /// all of it and deletes it would otherwise have the fold — and the editor
    /// they are typing in — vanish from under them mid-edit, which is the one
    /// keystroke in a body that must not close the thing it was typed into.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// Deliberately not every row. A fold on a row with no body would be this
    /// component offering "add a body" to "Ring the dentist", and whether a task
    /// may have one at all is the host's call rather than a list's.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private bool HasFold => Task.HasBody || _editingBody;

    /// <summary>Waiting on something outstanding. Read off the status rather
    /// than recomputed, so done-wins-over-a-missing-predecessor is decided in
    /// exactly one place.</summary>
    private bool Blocked => Status is { Readiness: TaskReadiness.Blocked };

    private string StateLabel => Task.Done ? "Done" : Blocked ? "Blocked" : "Not done";

    /// <summary>The clipboard text. Unchanged by editing, and correct while it is
    /// happening only because the body arrives here per keystroke: this reads
    /// <c>Task.Body</c>, which under a blur-commit editor would still be the text
    /// from before the reader started typing. Copy would then hand over a body the
    /// reader can see is out of date, in the one control whose entire job is to
    /// hand over exactly what is on screen.</summary>
    private string CopyText =>
        !string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(CopyValue) ? CopyValue
        : Task.HasBody ? $"{Task.Title}\n\n{Task.Body!.Trim()}"
        : Task.Title;

    /// <summary>The metadata line as this row draws it: what the chain worked
    /// out first, then what the task knows about itself. A row that cannot be
    /// started leads with why, because everything after that is moot until it is
    /// fixed.</summary>
    private IReadOnlyList<TaskDetail> Details => [.. ChainDetails, .. Task.Details];

    private IReadOnlyList<TaskDetail> ChainDetails
    {
        get
        {
            if (Status is null) return [];

            var details = new List<TaskDetail>();

            // Named, not counted. "Waiting on 2" tells the reader they are stuck
            // without telling them what to go and do about it.
            if (Status.Waiting.Count > 0)
            {
                details.Add(new TaskDetail(
                    TaskDetailKind.Blocked,
                    string.Join(", ", Status.Waiting.Select(dependency => dependency.Label))));
            }

            if (Status.InCycle) details.Add(new TaskDetail(TaskDetailKind.Cycle, "waits on itself"));

            return details;
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The body, parsed. Held between renders rather than parsed on each one: a
    /// row in a list re-renders whenever anything around it moves, and the
    /// markdown has not changed while it did.
    /// <para>
    /// ParseDocument, not Parse. The entry reading folds a <c>##</c> and
    /// everything under it into a sub-item for a host to lay out as its own
    /// card, and this row has nowhere to lay one out — a body with a sub-header
    /// in it would render its first paragraph and silently drop the rest. Here
    /// a heading is a heading, which is also what the person writing a prompt
    /// meant by it.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private IReadOnlyList<MdBlock> BodyBlocks
    {
        get
        {
            if (!string.Equals(_parsed, Task.Body, StringComparison.Ordinal))
            {
                _parsed = Task.Body;
                _blocks = MarkdownPreview.ParseDocument(Task.Body);
            }

            return _blocks ?? [];
        }
    }

    protected override void OnInitialized() => _expanded = BodyExpanded;

    /// <summary>
    /// Two answers to "what should the field show", and both of them have to be
    /// given as the parameters land rather than while they stand.
    /// <para>
    /// The always-open field is seeded from <c>Task.Title</c> whenever that changes
    /// and never otherwise, so the reader's half-typed draft survives every render
    /// the list around it causes. Reasserting the title on each render instead would
    /// delete a character a moment after it was typed, because a row in a list
    /// re-renders whenever anything near it moves.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// The list's request opens the editor when it has just changed, and only then,
    /// for the same reason read the other way round: a row that re-read a standing
    /// request would re-open the field — and reset the draft — under a reader who
    /// had already pressed Escape in it.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    protected override async Task OnParametersSetAsync()
    {
        SeedDirectDraft();

        if (Editing == _asked) return;

        _asked = Editing;

        // Already open, which is the pencil having got there first: the list is
        // only agreeing with what the row is doing. Re-opening would put the
        // title back over whatever has been typed since.
        if (Editing && !_editing) await StartRenameAsync();
    }

    /// <summary>The always-open field's draft, kept level with the host's title.
    /// Its own method because the seeding and the list's request are two unrelated
    /// answers that happen to be owed at the same moment, and reading them as one
    /// block invited the idea that one gates the other.</summary>
    private void SeedDirectDraft()
    {
        if (!DirectRename)
        {
            _seededFrom = null;
            return;
        }

        if (string.Equals(_seededFrom, Task.Title, StringComparison.Ordinal)) return;

        _seededFrom = Task.Title;
        _draft = Task.Title;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Renaming in place, rather than in the pane the row opens.
    /// <para>
    /// The draft is held here and the row is only told about it when it settles.
    /// A rename that reported every keystroke would put a save per character
    /// into whatever the host does with these — and would make Escape mean
    /// nothing, because the change it was meant to abandon had already landed.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private async Task StartRenameAsync()
    {
        // The pencil is not rendered without a listener, but the list can ask as
        // well, and the answer has to be the same either way.
        if (!CanRename) return;

        _draft = Task.Title;
        _editing = true;

        // Asked for here, done after the render. There is no field to focus at
        // this point in the method — opening the editor is a change to this row's
        // state, and the input it describes is a node some later batch creates.
        _focusRename = true;

        await EditingChanged.InvokeAsync(true);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Puts the caret in the field, once, after the render that created it.
    /// <para>
    /// Here rather than in <see cref="StartRenameAsync"/> because this is the one
    /// point Blazor guarantees runs with the batch already applied. Focusing where
    /// the editor opens read correctly and was not: the render had only been
    /// queued, so the id resolved to nothing or to the node the next batch
    /// replaced — while the guard call one statement behind it, arriving after that
    /// batch, landed on the field that survived. The symptom was a field that was
    /// plainly there, with the focus on nothing and the caret at the end of the
    /// old title.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// Once per opening, which is what the flag is for and not a detail. A row
    /// re-renders whenever anything in the list moves, and a focus on every render
    /// would drag the caret back over the title while somebody was typing.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// Putting the ring back on the pencil after a dismissal is the same problem
    /// again and so it is the same mechanism: the button does not exist while the
    /// field is open, so the earliest anything can focus it is the render that
    /// brought it back.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (_focusRename)
        {
            _focusRename = false;

            await FocusRenameAsync();

            if (QuickEdit) await GuardTabAsync();
        }

        if (_focusEdit)
        {
            _focusEdit = false;

            await RestoreFocusAsync();
        }
    }

    /// <summary>The caret in the field, with the title selected so the first
    /// keystroke replaces it rather than being added to the end of it. One rule for
    /// both ways in: a reader who reached for the pencil reached for it to change
    /// what is there, exactly as a reader tabbing down the list did.</summary>
    private Task FocusRenameAsync() => QuickEditField.FocusAsync(Js, RenameFieldId);

    /// <summary>Stops the browser moving the focus on while this field has it, so
    /// Tab is the list's key rather than the browser's for as long as the editor is
    /// open. Both directions: forward hands the editor down the list and Shift+Tab
    /// hands it back up, so neither is the browser's to answer.</summary>
    private Task GuardTabAsync() => QuickEditField.GuardTabAsync(Js, RenameFieldId);

    /// <summary>The focus ring back on the pencil the field was opened from, with
    /// nothing selected — it is a button, and there is nothing on it to select.
    /// <para>
    /// A dismissal restores the focus to its trigger, which is the rule for every
    /// other thing in the product that can be dismissed, and it is what stops
    /// Escape being a dead end: without it the field that had the focus is simply
    /// gone, the ring lands on the document body, and a reader using the keyboard
    /// has to tab in from the top of the page to get back to the row they were
    /// standing on.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private Task RestoreFocusAsync() => QuickEditField.FocusAsync(Js, _editId, select: false);

    private async Task OnRenameKeyAsync(KeyboardEventArgs e)
    {
        if (e.Key == "Escape")
        {
            // Abandon: the row goes back to what it said, and nothing is raised.
            // In direct mode there is nothing to close, so putting it back means
            // putting the title back into the field rather than emptying it.
            if (DirectRename)
            {
                _draft = Task.Title;
                return;
            }

            // Otherwise the field closes, and closing it ends a quick edit as
            // well — which is the only thing here that is about the chain rather
            // than this row: a reader who has just thrown a title away did not
            // mean "and now the next one".
            //
            // And the focus goes back to the pencil, which is where it came from.
            // Not a quick-edit rule: it is the same restore-to-the-trigger a
            // dismissal owes anywhere, and it happens to be what keeps the end of
            // a chain from dropping the reader onto the document body.
            _editing = false;
            _draft = string.Empty;
            _focusEdit = CanRename;
            await EditingChanged.InvokeAsync(false);
            return;
        }

        // Settled, and on to the next row — or off the end of the list, which
        // ends it. Which row that is is not this row's to decide: the id goes out
        // with the direction, and the list answers with a row or with nothing.
        //
        // Read before Enter rather than after it so that the two keys stay one
        // decision each: this one settles the rename and hands it on, that one
        // settles it and stops.
        if (e.Key == "Tab" && QuickEdit)
        {
            // Taken before the commit, which is the last moment this row is
            // certainly still the row the reader was in.
            var from = Task.Id;

            await CommitRenameAsync();
            await OnEditMove.InvokeAsync(new TaskEditMove(from, !e.ShiftKey));
            return;
        }

        if (e.Key == "Enter")
        {
            await CommitRenameAsync();
            return;
        }

        // Alt+Arrow reorders from inside the field too, and it has to: with the
        // title always a field there is no line left to press it on, and the row's
        // own draggable is off. Alt+Up and Alt+Down mean nothing to a caret on
        // Windows, so nothing is being taken away to get this — which is exactly
        // why the keyboard route is the one that survives an always-open input.
        await TryKeyboardMoveAsync(e);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Alt+Arrow and Alt+Shift+Arrow, wherever either was pressed. Shared by the
    /// row's line and by the rename field, because "can this row be picked up"
    /// has one answer and a second copy of the test would be a second chance for
    /// the two to disagree about it.
    /// <para>
    /// Shift is the whole difference between moving this row and making it wait
    /// for a neighbour, and the link is tested first so that one press can only
    /// ever do one of them. Chosen as the learnable sibling of Alt+Arrow;
    /// Ctrl+Shift+Arrow was rejected outright, because it is select-by-word in
    /// the rename field that shares this handler.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// With nobody listening for links — or on a row nothing can wait for —
    /// Alt+Shift+Arrow falls through and still moves the row, which is exactly
    /// what it did before this existed. A list that has not asked for links does
    /// not quietly lose a chord it already had.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private async Task TryKeyboardMoveAsync(KeyboardEventArgs e)
    {
        if (!e.AltKey) return;

        var delta = e.Key switch
        {
            "ArrowUp" => -1,
            "ArrowDown" => 1,
            _ => 0
        };

        if (delta == 0) return;

        // Not gated on Draggable: that folds in whether the list reorders at all,
        // and a chained list that does not reorder must still be linkable.
        //
        // Not gated on Done either, and that is a fix rather than an oversight.
        // A finished row draws no link handle, so it was tempting to refuse the
        // chord here too — but refusing here meant falling through to the reorder
        // branch, which declines a finished row as well, and the press vanished
        // without a word. Every other refusal on this gesture says why, so this
        // one is reported and the list answers it.
        if (e.ShiftKey && OnKeyboardLink.HasDelegate)
        {
            await OnKeyboardLink.InvokeAsync(delta);
            return;
        }

        // Gated on Draggable, which reads as "can be picked up" rather than as
        // anything about pointers — so one predicate covers every gesture.
        if (!Draggable || !OnKeyboardMove.HasDelegate) return;

        await OnKeyboardMove.InvokeAsync(delta);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Alt+Arrow asks the list to move this row.
    /// <para>
    /// Alt and not a bare arrow. A plain ArrowUp on a focused row is how someone
    /// reads down a long list, and a row that swallowed it to reorder itself
    /// would take away scrolling to add moving. Alt is also the one modifier with
    /// no default action on a button, so nothing has to be prevented — and
    /// preventing selectively is not expressible here anyway, since Blazor
    /// resolves preventDefault when the row renders rather than when the key
    /// arrives.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// Nothing is raised when no list is listening, on the same terms as the
    /// circle and the body editor: a row does not offer a control it cannot
    /// honour.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private async Task OnRowKeyDownAsync(KeyboardEventArgs e)
    {
        if (e.Key is "Tab")
        {
            // Backward off the line only when the line is the front of the row.
            // With a circle in front of it, Shift+Tab belongs to the circle.
            if (e.ShiftKey)
            {
                if (!CanToggle) await OnTabBackward.InvokeAsync();
            }
            else
            {
                await OnTabForward.InvokeAsync();
            }

            return;
        }

        await TryKeyboardMoveAsync(e);
    }

    /// <summary>The circle hears one key, and only because it is the row's first
    /// tab stop: Shift+Tab is the way back out of a row somebody was tabbed into.
    /// Everything else about the circle is still a click.</summary>
    private async Task OnCheckKeyDownAsync(KeyboardEventArgs e)
    {
        if (e.Key is "Tab" && e.ShiftKey) await OnTabBackward.InvokeAsync();
    }

    private async Task CommitRenameAsync()
    {
        if (!RenameOpen) return;

        var title = _draft.Trim();

        if (DirectRename)
        {
            // Nothing to close: the field is the title. An empty one goes back to
            // what the row says rather than sitting there blank — in this mode the
            // field is the only place the title appears, so leaving it empty would
            // read as a row that had lost its name.
            if (title.Length == 0) _draft = Task.Title;
        }
        else
        {
            _editing = false;
            _draft = string.Empty;
            await EditingChanged.InvokeAsync(false);
        }

        // An empty title would leave a row nobody can identify, and a title that
        // did not change is not a rename.
        if (title.Length == 0 || string.Equals(title, Task.Title, StringComparison.Ordinal)) return;

        await OnRename.InvokeAsync(new TaskRename(Task.Id, title));
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Reported, not kept. The row does not hold the text it was typed — the host
    /// owns the body, so the next render reads the host's value back out of
    /// <c>Task</c>, and a copy held here would be a second version of the same
    /// string waiting to disagree with it.
    /// <para>
    /// What the reader is looking at meanwhile is the textarea's own content,
    /// which is the browser's and is what keeps the caret where they left it. The
    /// promise this row makes is narrower and more useful than "it re-reads every
    /// keystroke": it never holds a body of its own, so the host's value wins the
    /// moment the two differ.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private Task OnBodyTypedAsync(string value) =>
        OnBodyChanged.InvokeAsync(new TaskBodyChange(Task.Id, value));

    /// <summary>
    /// The gutter box, pressed.
    /// <para>
    /// What is reported is the state the box now reads as, worked out from the
    /// parameter rather than from the element: the host owns the selection, so
    /// <c>!SelectionChecked</c> is what the browser has just done to the box and
    /// what the host is being asked to agree with. The row holds nothing of its
    /// own, exactly as it holds no body — which is what keeps the tick the reader
    /// sees and the set the host is acting on the same fact.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// Heard on click rather than on change so the modifier survives. Shift is
    /// the difference between one row and eight, and it is on
    /// <c>MouseEventArgs</c> only.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private Task OnSelectionClickAsync(MouseEventArgs e) =>
        SelectionChanged.InvokeAsync(
            new TaskSelectionToggle(Task.Id, !SelectionChecked, e.ShiftKey));

    private string ClassValue => string.Join(' ', new[]
    {
        "task-item",
        Task.Done ? "task-item--done" : null,
        // The row wraps for the region under it, which outlasts the body while an
        // edit is emptying it.
        HasFold ? "task-item--has-body" : null,
        Blocked ? "task-item--blocked" : null,
        Next ? "task-item--next" : null,
        Selected ? "task-item--selected" : null,
        // Picked, not open. Two states with two meanings, so two classes:
        // .design/interaction-guidelines.md#focus-and-selection asks for
        // selection to be distinct from hover and from focus, and borrowing the
        // open row's paint would make it indistinguishable from a third thing.
        CanSelect && SelectionChecked ? "task-item--picked" : null,
        Dragging ? "task-item--dragging" : null,
        // What this row is to the link drag in flight. On the row rather than on
        // anything inside it, because the row is the drop target: there is nothing
        // smaller than this to aim at, and nothing smaller to say yes or no.
        LinkRole switch
        {
            TaskLinkRole.Source => "task-item--link-source",
            TaskLinkRole.Target => "task-item--link-target",
            TaskLinkRole.Refused => "task-item--link-refused",
            _ => null
        },
        // Crossed with the role rather than folded into it: armed says the pointer
        // is here, and the role says what will happen if it lets go here.
        LinkArmed && LinkRole is not TaskLinkRole.None and not TaskLinkRole.Source
            ? "task-item--link-armed"
            : null,
        CssClass
    }.Where(part => !string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(part)));

    private string? Suffix(string part) => TestId is null ? null : $"{TestId}-{part}";
}
